\b HRMS Backend Docker Setup Steps\b0\par
\par
This document explains how to run the HRMS backend entirely from a prebuilt Docker image, without installing Python packages on the local system.\par
\par
\b Goal\b0\par
Run the backend code from Docker image \i iscuser1/rps-api-python312-deps:latest\i0  and keep all Python dependencies inside the container.\par
\par
\b Prerequisites\b0\par
1. Docker Desktop is installed and running.\par
2. The backend source code exists in \i D:\\Project\\HRMS\\Backend\i0 .\par
3. The Docker image \i iscuser1/rps-api-python312-deps:latest\i0  is already available locally or can be pulled.\par
4. Ports 8000, 5432, and 6379 are free on the machine.\par
\par
\b Why this approach is used\b0\par
Normally, a backend project installs Python and all packages on the system or inside a virtual environment. In this setup, that is avoided. Instead:\par
1. The backend source code stays on the local machine.\par
2. The code is mounted into a Docker container.\par
3. Python 3.12 and all required packages come from the Docker image.\par
4. The app runs inside the container, not from the host system.\par
\par
\b Step 1: Open the backend folder\b0\par
Project path:\par
\i D:\\Project\\HRMS\\Backend\i0\par
\par
\b Step 2: Create a Docker Compose file\b0\par
Create a file named \b docker-compose.yml\b0  in the backend root.\par
\par
Put this content in it:\par
\par
\f0\fs20
services:\par
  backend:\par
    image: iscuser1/rps-api-python312-deps:latest\par
    container_name: hrms-backend\par
    working_dir: /workspace\par
    env_file:\par
      - .env\par
    ports:\par
      - "8000:8000"\par
      - "5678:5678"\par
    volumes:\par
      - .:/workspace\par
    environment:\par
      - PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1\par
    command: ["python", "-m", "app.StartServer"]\par
    depends_on:\par
      - postgres\par
      - redis\par
\par
  postgres:\par
    image: postgres:16\par
    container_name: hrms-postgres\par
    environment:\par
      POSTGRES_DB: hrms\par
      POSTGRES_USER: postgres\par
      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: postgres\par
    ports:\par
      - "5432:5432"\par
    volumes:\par
      - postgres_data:/var/lib/postgresql/data\par
\par
  redis:\par
    image: redis:7\par
    container_name: hrms-redis\par
    ports:\par
      - "6379:6379"\par
    volumes:\par
      - redis_data:/data\par
\par
volumes:\par
  postgres_data:\par
  redis_data:\par
\fs24
\par
\b Explanation of this file\b0\par
1. \b backend\b0  uses the prebuilt dependency image.\par
2. \b working_dir: /workspace\b0  sets the folder inside the container.\par
3. \b .:/workspace\b0  mounts the backend source code into the container.\par
4. \b command\b0  starts the backend using the project startup module.\par
5. \b postgres\b0  and \b redis\b0  are helper containers used by the backend.\par
\par
\b Step 3: Create the environment file\b0\par
Create a file named \b .env\b0  in the same folder.\par
\par
Put this content in it:\par
\par
\f0\fs20
APP_NAME=HRMS Backend\par
APP_VERSION=1.0.0\par
DEBUG=false\par
DEBUG_MODE=false\par
LOG_LEVEL=INFO\par
API_V1_PREFIX=/api/v1\par
\par
ENABLE_SSO=false\par
ENABLE_REDIS=false\par
ENABLE_RATE_LIMIT=false\par
ENABLE_AUTH_MIDDLEWARE=false\par
ENABLE_REQUEST_LOGGING=true\par
ENABLE_CORS=true\par
ENABLE_SWAGGER=true\par
ENABLE_CSRF_PROTECTION=true\par
ENABLE_PAYLOAD_ENCRYPTION=true\par
\par
DATABASE_HOST=postgres\par
DATABASE_PORT=5432\par
DATABASE_USER=postgres\par
DATABASE_PASSWORD=postgres\par
DATABASE_NAME=hrms\par
\par
REDIS_HOST=redis\par
REDIS_PORT=6379\par
REDIS_DB=0\par
REDIS_MAX_CONNECTIONS=20\par
\par
ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000\par
HRMS_CSRF_SECRET_KEY=zE8nLiA0tePsr686Jp6idRKhjpOfH0rPuYcVoJyPUUn=\par
SESSION_TIMEOUT_MINUTES=60\par
\par
CORS_ALLOW_ORIGINS=*\par
CORS_ALLOW_CREDENTIALS=true\par
CORS_ALLOW_METHODS=GET,POST,PUT,PATCH,DELETE,OPTIONS\par
CORS_ALLOW_HEADERS=*\par
\fs24
\par
\b Why these values are important\b0\par
1. \b DATABASE_HOST=postgres\b0  points the backend to the PostgreSQL container, not the host system.\par
2. \b REDIS_HOST=redis\b0  points the backend to the Redis container.\par
3. \b ENABLE_SSO=false\b0  and \b ENABLE_AUTH_MIDDLEWARE=false\b0  make local startup easier when Azure settings are not configured yet.\par
4. Swagger is enabled so API testing can be done from the browser.\par
\par
\b Step 4: Make sure the backend startup command exists\b0\par
The compose file starts the backend with:\par
\b python -m app.StartServer\b0\par
\par
That means the project should contain a Python module at:\par
\i app\\StartServer.py\i0\par
\par
This startup module should launch Uvicorn for the FastAPI app, usually something like:\par
\b app.main:app\b0\par
\par
If the project does not use \b app.StartServer\b0 , then update the compose \b command\b0  to match the correct startup command for the project.\par
\par
\b Step 5: Start the containers\b0\par
Open a terminal in:\par
\i D:\\Project\\HRMS\\Backend\i0\par
\par
Run:\par
\b docker compose up -d\b0\par
\par
What happens here:\par
1. PostgreSQL container starts.\par
2. Redis container starts.\par
3. Backend container starts from the dependency image.\par
4. The backend code is read from the mounted local project folder.\par
5. No Python packages are installed on the host machine.\par
\par
\b Step 6: Check running containers\b0\par
Run:\par
\b docker compose ps\b0\par
\par
Expected result:\par
1. \b hrms-backend\b0  should be Up.\par
2. \b hrms-postgres\b0  should be Up.\par
3. \b hrms-redis\b0  should be Up.\par
\par
\b Step 7: Check backend logs\b0\par
Run:\par
\b docker compose logs -f backend\b0\par
\par
Expected log lines should include messages similar to:\par
1. Application startup complete.\par
2. Uvicorn running on http://0.0.0.0:8000\par
\par
\b Step 8: Check from browser\b0\par
Open a browser and use these URLs:\par
1. \b http://localhost:8000/health\b0\par
2. \b http://localhost:8000/docs\b0\par
3. \b http://localhost:8000/redoc\b0\par
\par
Expected health response:\par
\b {"status":"healthy","database":"connected","redis":"disabled"}\b0\par
\par
\b Step 9: Stop the containers\b0\par
Run:\par
\b docker compose down\b0\par
\par
If database and Redis data should also be removed, run:\par
\b docker compose down -v\b0\par
\par
\b Step 10: Common troubleshooting\b0\par
1. If \b .env not found\b0  appears, create the \b .env\b0  file in the backend root.\par
2. If port 8000 is busy, change the mapping in \b docker-compose.yml\b0  from \b 8000:8000\b0  to another host port such as \b 8001:8000\b0 .\par
3. If the backend container exits immediately, check backend logs with \b docker compose logs backend\b0 .\par
4. If database connection fails, confirm \b DATABASE_HOST=postgres\b0  and the Postgres container is running.\par
5. If Swagger does not open, confirm \b ENABLE_SWAGGER=true\b0 .\par
\par
\b Final summary\b0\par
This setup works by separating code and dependencies:\par
1. Source code stays local.\par
2. Dependencies stay inside Docker image \i iscuser1/rps-api-python312-deps:latest\i0 .\par
3. Docker Compose connects backend, database, and Redis together.\par
4. The host system does not need Python package installation for this backend.\par
\par
\b Files created for this setup\b0\par
1. \b docker-compose.yml\b0\par
2. \b .env\b0\par
\par
\b Useful commands\b0\par
1. \b docker compose up -d\b0\par
2. \b docker compose ps\b0\par
3. \b docker compose logs -f backend\b0\par
4. \b docker compose down\b0\par
}
